======================================================================================================================
K1 (exact-relations compute) -- the outer-curve shape law:  2 dlnv/dlnr = 1 + (1+L(y)) dln g_bar/dln r
======================================================================================================================

  (A) THE RESTATEMENT TEST, EXECUTED.  Is the relation new content, or the RAR differentiated?
      Build synthetic rotation curves that obey g_obs = nu(g_bar/a_0) g_bar EXACTLY, with an arbitrary
      baryonic profile, and measure both sides of the relation numerically.  If it is an identity the
      residual is machine noise and the candidate carries no information the RAR does not already have.
      a_0 = 9.360e-11  profile point             max |LHS - RHS| = 1.139e-07
      a_0 = 9.360e-11  profile exponential       max |LHS - RHS| = 6.181e-08
      a_0 = 9.360e-11  profile flat-then-fall    max |LHS - RHS| = 1.128e-07
      a_0 = 1.130e-10  profile point             max |LHS - RHS| = 1.139e-07
      a_0 = 1.130e-10  profile exponential       max |LHS - RHS| = 5.740e-08
      a_0 = 1.130e-10  profile flat-then-fall    max |LHS - RHS| = 1.083e-07
      a_0 = 3.000e-10  profile point             max |LHS - RHS| = 1.139e-07
      a_0 = 3.000e-10  profile exponential       max |LHS - RHS| = 3.813e-08
      a_0 = 3.000e-10  profile flat-then-fall    max |LHS - RHS| = 8.503e-08
  [PASS] the relation is an EXACT identity of the RAR (residual is numerical, not physical)   (worst residual over 9 synthetic curves = 1.14e-07 (finite-difference noise))
      ==> is_restatement = TRUE.  It is not a restatement of v^4 = G M_b a_0 -- the BTFR predicts
      dln v/dln r = 0 identically and nothing else -- but it IS the radial acceleration relation
      differentiated in ln r.  Every number it can produce is a number the RAR already contains.
      Criterion (5) therefore fails at one remove: the candidate is a re-parameterisation of the FIRST
      law, not a second one.  What it could still be is a Upsilon-free ESTIMATOR of a_0 -- which is the
      only claim worth testing, and is tested below.

  (B) SPARC.  Outer window = the outer 50% of points, >= 5 points, radial range >= 1.25x.
      131 galaxies with a measurable outer window
      baryonic shape slope over the window: median -1.36, 16-84% -1.87 to -0.31  (a point mass is exactly -2.00)

      model                                        rms      median resid   regression   r
      Route A, canonical a_0 = 9.36e-11           0.1689       -0.0397        0.725 +0.728
      Route A, alt a_0 = 1.13e-10                 0.1688       -0.0460        0.736 +0.728
      NEWTONIAN alternative (nu = 1, L = 0)       0.3253       +0.2369        0.438 +0.715
      pure deep MOND (L = -1/2)                   0.1844       -0.1207        0.876 +0.715
      BTFR / flat-curve null (dlnv/dlnr = 0)      0.2501       +0.0546      (const)    n/a
  [PASS] Route A beats the Newtonian alternative   (0.1689 vs 0.3253)
  [PASS] Route A beats the flat-curve (BTFR) null   (0.1689 vs 0.2501)
  [FAIL] law scatter <= 0.10 in dln v/dln r (RAR-class)   (rms 0.1689)
      chi2/N against the formal slope errors (median 0.0608): 32.9  -> the residual is 2.8x the errors, so the scatter is REAL, not noise

