======================================================================================================================
  VERDICT ON K1
======================================================================================================================
  (1) measured quantities?  YES -- v(r), r and the baryonic profile's log-slope.
  (2) a_0 with a PREDICTED coefficient?  the relation is parameter-free, but see (5).
  (3) RAR-class scatter?  NO -- rms 0.169 in dln v/dln r against the <= 0.10 asked for, and chi2/N = 33 says it is real.
  (4) unstated?  the identity itself overlaps another agent's k02_rar_slope_law.py this session, and
      the local-slope test is the repo's own item 22 / item 115.
  (5) restatement?  TRUE at one remove, and PROVEN above rather than argued: it is the radial
      acceleration relation differentiated in ln r, satisfied to the 1e-7 finite-difference floor by
      construction on nine synthetic curves at three different a_0.  It carries no
      information the first law does not already contain.
  THE a_0 IT RETURNS: 1.122e-10 +- 0.125 dex statistical, the alt footing to 0.003 dex --
  and that agreement is not evidence, because d log a_0/d log Upsilon = +1.70 means the same
  pipeline returns 5.6e-11 at Upsilon = 0.33 and 2.2e-10 at Upsilon = 0.75.  The number is chosen by
  the M/L convention, not measured.
  THE STRICT UPSILON-FREE FORM, which is the only version that would have escaped that, returns
  4.47e-10 -- +0.68 dex from canonical, +0.60 from alt -- with a correlation of +0.24 against its own
  prediction.  It collapses.
  ==> CANDIDATE K1 IS NOT A SECOND LAW.  Its only independent value would have been as a Upsilon-free
      a_0 estimator, and the sections above measure how well that works: it does not.

